**Lemma 4.1 (Takagi conventions and independence of the factor).** For a
complex symmetric $C$ there is a unitary $U$ with
$C=U\operatorname{diag}(\tau_i)U^T$, where $\tau_i\geq0$ are the
singular values. If $X=AA^\dagger\succeq0$ with $A$ of full column rank
$r=\operatorname{rank}X$, the singular values of $A^TB_0A$ depend only
on $X$, with zeros added when necessary. At $X=0$ use an empty factor.

Proof. This is the mature Takagi factorization; its needed finite proof
also fixes the transpose convention. Let the antilinear map
$\mathcal T v=C\overline v$. Symmetry gives
$\mathcal T^2=CC^\dagger$ and
$\|\mathcal T v\|^2=v^\dagger CC^\dagger v$. It preserves each eigenspace
of $CC^\dagger$. On a positive eigenspace of eigenvalue $\lambda$,
$\mathcal T/\sqrt\lambda$ is a norm-preserving antilinear involution.
Every vector is the sum of a fixed vector and $i$ times a fixed vector:
use $(v+\mathcal T v/\sqrt\lambda)/2$ and
$(v-\mathcal T v/\sqrt\lambda)/(2i)$. Inner products of fixed vectors
are real. Real Gram–Schmidt therefore gives an orthonormal fixed basis
whose complex span is the eigenspace. On the zero eigenspace
$\mathcal T=0$. Combining these bases gives unitary columns $u_i$ with
$C\overline{u_i}=\tau_i u_i$, and hence the claimed factorization.
Their squares are the eigenvalues of $CC^\dagger$, so these are precisely
the singular values, including multiplicities and zeros.

Two minimal factors of the same $X$ have the same range. Write
$A'=AR$ using a left inverse of $A$; $AA^\dagger=A' A'^\dagger$ gives
$RR^\dagger=I_r$, and the square matrix $R$ is unitary. Thus
$C'=R^TCR$ has the same singular values. For a nonminimal factor use
$A'=AQ$ with $QQ^\dagger=I_r$. Extend the orthonormal rows of $Q$ to a
unitary matrix. Unitary congruence then identifies $Q^TCQ$ with $C$
plus a zero block. Consequently a minimal factor and a list padded to
length three suffice, even if another factor has more columns. No
physical conjugation operation is invoked. $\square$

